          "evidence": ["speech-components"]
        },
        {
          "key": "count-adjacencies",
          "text": "An undirected edge is stored in both endpoints' adjacency lists. With \\(m\\) undirected edges, a full scan examines \\(2m\\) entries.",
          "evidence": ["speech-adjacencies"]
        }
      ],
      "highlights": [
        {
          "key": "first-discovery",
          "title": "Tracing the queue to prove first discovery is shortest",
          "interval": {
            "start_s": 760,
            "end_s": 1145
          },
          "teaches": "In an unweighted graph BFS reaches each vertex first along a path with the fewest edges, because the queue only ever holds distances \\(d\\) and \\(d+1\\).",
          "points": [
            "Removing a vertex at distance \\(d\\) gives each undiscovered neighbour distance \\(d+1\\).",
            "On the path \\(A\\)–\\(B\\)–\\(C\\) the distances from \\(A\\) are \\(0\\), \\(1\\) and \\(2\\)."
          ],
          "sources": [
            "components"
          ],
          "pages": "1",
          "evidence": [
            "speech-first",
            "frame-first"
          ],
